1.text:
In $ {\mathbb R}^{n}$ write the $n$ random vertices in the canonical basis

\begin{equation*}
V_{i}=X_{i}e_{i}\qquad i=1,\dots ,n ,\qquad 
X_{i}\stackrel{i.i.d}{\sim}{\rm Unif}[-1,1] ,
\end{equation*}
and put $V_{0}=0$.  On the $i$-th axis $|X_{i}|$ is ${\rm Unif}[0,1]$,
so that

\begin{equation*}
 {\rm Vol}_{n}\bigl({\rm conv}\{0,V_{1},\dots ,V_{n}\}\bigr)
     =\frac1{n!}\Bigl|\det [V_{1}\dots V_{n}]\Bigr|
     =\frac1{n!}\prod_{i=1}^{n}|X_{i}| ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}\Bigl[{\rm Vol}_{n}\Bigr]=\frac1{n!}\prod_{i=1}^{n}
{\mathbb E}|X_{i}|=\frac{1}{n!\,2^{\,n}},\qquad n\ge 1,
\end{equation*}
in particular ${\mathbb E}{\rm Vol}_{1}=1/2$.  For two different
axes

\begin{equation*}
\|V_{i}-V_{j}\|^{2}=X_{i}^{2}+X_{j}^{2}, \qquad i\ne j,
\end{equation*}
and with $X\sim {\rm Unif}[-1,1]$

\begin{equation*}
D_{n}:={\mathbb E}\|V_{i}-V_{j}\|
   =4\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}\,dxdy
   =J .
\end{equation*}
Putting $s=\mathop{{\rm arsinh}}1=\ln\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)$,

\begin{equation*}
J=\int_{0}^{1}\!\Bigl[\tfrac12\sqrt{1+y^{2}}
       +\tfrac{y^{2}}2\ln\frac{1+\sqrt{1+y^{2}}}{y}\Bigr]dy
   =\frac{s+\sqrt2}{3},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D_{n}=D=\frac{\,\ln\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)+\sqrt2\,}{3}
      \approx0.765196 ,
\end{equation*}
the mean radius of a point uniformly distributed in the square
$[-1,1]^{2}$.  In particular ${\mathbb E}\|V_{i}\|={\mathbb E}|X_{i}|=1/2$.

For the $N=n+1$ points $\{0,V_{i}\}$ the mean distance of an unordered
pair is

\begin{equation*}
\bar d_{n}
     =\frac{\,n\frac12+\binom n2D\,}{\binom{n+1}2}
     =\frac{1+(n-1)D}{\,n+1\,},
\qquad \bar d_{1}=\tfrac12,
\end{equation*}
and with $R_{n}:={\mathbb E}{\rm Vol}_{n}/\bar d_{n}$

\begin{equation*}
R_{n}
   =\frac{n+1}{1+(n-1)D}\;\frac{2^{-n}}{n!},\qquad n\ge1,
\end{equation*}
$R_{1}=1$.  Hence in closed form

\begin{equation*}
S_{N}:=\sum_{n=1}^{N}R_{n}
     =\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{n+1}{1+(n-1)D}\frac{2^{-n}}{n!}
\end{equation*}
and $S_{\infty}$ converges essentially at $n=2,3$.  With
$D=(s+\sqrt2)/3$,

\[
S_{\infty}=1+\frac{3}{1+D}\frac{2^{-2}}{2!}
          +\frac{4}{1+2D}\frac{2^{-3}}{3!}
          +\frac{5}{1+3D}\frac{2^{-4}}{4!}+\cdots
          \approx1.2497\approx1.25 ,
\]

the first three terms already giving $1.245$.  In particular
$R_{1}=1.000,R_{2}\approx0.212,R_{3}\approx0.033,R_{4}\approx0.004$,
etc.

Remark.  If the mean inter–point distance $D$ itself is used as the
normalising distance, i.e. $R^{\ast}_{n}= {\mathbb E}{\rm Vol}_{n}/D$,
then

\[
R^{\ast}_{n}= \frac{2^{-n}}{n!\,D},\qquad 
\sum_{n\ge1}R^{\ast}_{n}= \frac{e^{1/2}-1}{D}
      =\frac{3\bigl(e^{1/2}-1\bigr)}
             {\ln\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)+\sqrt2}
      \approx0.848,
\]

$0.8478$ with the closed form $D=(s+\sqrt2)/3$.  This is the usual
point–point normalisation $ {\mathbb E}\|V_{i}-V_{j}\|=D$.
The origin–point normalisation ${\mathbb E}\|V_{i}\|=1/2$ gives the
familiar $ \sum_{n\ge1}2^{1-n}/n!=2\!\left(e^{1/2}-1\right)\approx1.297$,
i.e. the $ \bar d_{n}\to D$ limit quoted above.

